Object.assign(window.deepArticles,{
  "fijian-language":southLanguageArticle({title:"Fijian language",infoboxKicker:"Austronesian language of the iTaukei people",infoboxTitle:"Fijian (Vosa Vakaviti)",
    lead:"Fijian, or <em>Vosa Vakaviti</em>, is the principal indigenous language of Fiji and the ancestral language of the iTaukei people. Numerous communal and regional varieties coexist with a standard based historically on Bauan speech. It remains central to village, church, chiefly, land, radio, and cultural life, while Japanese controls senior government and most imperial higher education.",
    canon:"Fijian classification, dialect diversity, Bauan standard, mission literacy, church use, and indigenous identity are historical. Its position under Japanese rule, bilingual schooling, terminology planning, and 1985 pressures are extrapolations. Speaker totals, curriculum hours, legal wording, and endangerment designation remain open.",
    sections:[{id:"classification",title:"Classification and name",html:"<p>Fijian belongs to the Oceanic branch of the Austronesian language family. <em>Vosa Vakaviti</em> means Fijian speech or language and connects the language directly with indigenous Fijian identity.</p>"},{id:"varieties",title:"Communal and regional varieties",html:"<p>Speech varies across islands, provinces, and communities. Western varieties and those of outlying islands may differ substantially from the eastern speech that colonial and mission institutions elevated.</p>"},{id:"bauan",title:"The Bauan standard",html:"<p>Missionaries developed a written standard principally from Bauan, whose chiefly prestige and eastern networks made it influential. Schooling, scripture, broadcasting, and administration spread that standard beyond Bau.</p>"},{id:"oral",title:"Oratory and performed knowledge",html:"<p>Chiefly address, genealogy, land history, sermons, poetry, song, ceremony, humor, reconciliation, and everyday etiquette depend on registers that literal translation cannot reproduce.</p>"},{id:"literacy",title:"Mission literacy and print",html:"<p>Christian missions established Latin-script literacy through scripture, hymnals, schools, letters, and newspapers. Churches remain major producers of Fijian speech and writing under Japanese supervision.</p>"},{id:"contact",title:"Contact with Fiji Hindi and Japanese",html:"<p>Fiji's plural society produces borrowing and multilingualism involving Fiji Hindi and English. Japanese supplies postwar vocabulary for government, technology, military service, higher schooling, and imperial employment.</p>"},{id:"education",title:"Education and broadcasting",html:"<p>Early schooling, cultural studies, church education, radio, and provincial media use Fijian. Examinations and advanced study favor Japanese, creating uneven access for rural students and pressure on urban family transmission.</p>"},{id:"1985",title:"Fijian in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 Fijian remains vigorous across most iTaukei communities and is widely visible in public culture. The main concern is domain inequality rather than immediate disappearance: the language can govern village life but seldom the state that governs Fiji.</p>"}],
    related:[{href:"itaukei-people.html",kicker:"Principal speaker community",label:"iTaukei People"},{href:"fiji-under-japanese-rule.html",kicker:"Principal territory",label:"Fiji under Japanese Rule"},{href:"suva.html",kicker:"Broadcast and education center",label:"Suva"},{href:"fiji-hindi-language.html",kicker:"Major fellow language",label:"Fiji Hindi"},{href:"japanese-language-micronesia.html",kicker:"Imperial high language",label:"Japanese Language in Micronesia"},{href:"education-japanese-micronesia.html",kicker:"Comparable island schooling",label:"Education in Japanese Micronesia"}],
    facts:[["Local name","Vosa Vakaviti"],["Language family","Austronesian · Oceanic"],["Principal people","iTaukei"],["Standard basis","Bauan"],["Writing system","Latin alphabet"],["Major domains","Village, church, chiefs, land, radio, and culture"],["Principal high-language competitor","Japanese"],["1985 condition","Vigorous, with major domain inequality"]],sources:[{href:"https://www.fiji.gov.fj/Media-Centre/Speeches/HE-PRESIDENT-JIOJI-KONROTE-S-SPEECH-AT-THE-FIRST-C",label:"Government of Fiji — recognition of iTaukei language and cultural inheritance"}]
  }),
  "fiji-hindi-language":southLanguageArticle({title:"Fiji Hindi",infoboxKicker:"Indo-Aryan language of Fiji",infoboxTitle:"Fiji Hindi (Fiji Baat)",
    lead:"Fiji Hindi, also called Fiji Baat, is the principal community language of Indo-Fijians. It formed from the northern Indian languages carried through indenture, especially Awadhi and Bhojpuri-related speech, together with Hindustani and contact vocabulary. It is distinct from standardized Hindi and remains the language of home, humor, popular culture, local commerce, and much intergenerational Indo-Fijian life.",
    canon:"Fiji Hindi's indenture origins, Indo-Aryan basis, distinction from Standard Hindi, and community role are historical. Japanese contact, school hierarchy, broadcasting, script policy, and 1985 domain pressures are extrapolations. Speaker totals, official status, orthographic rules, and curriculum remain open.",
    sections:[{id:"origins",title:"Language of girmit",html:"<p>Indentured workers from several regions needed a common vernacular on plantations and in settlements. Their speech converged into a Fijian form of Hindustani carrying shared memory of <em>girmit</em>.</p>"},{id:"sources",title:"Linguistic sources",html:"<p>Awadhi, Bhojpuri, and related northern Indian varieties supplied much of the grammar and vocabulary, with influence from broader Hindustani and later Standard Hindi and Urdu.</p>"},{id:"contact",title:"Pacific contact",html:"<p>Fijian and English contributed words for local plants, foods, land, work, government, and social relations. Japanese adds a further layer after conquest, especially in schooling, technology, and administration.</p>"},{id:"standard",title:"Fiji Hindi and Standard Hindi",html:"<p>Religious and literary education often privileges Standard Hindi or Urdu, while everyday speech remains Fiji Hindi. Treating the vernacular as corrupted standard speech can stigmatize the language most children actually acquire.</p>"},{id:"scripts",title:"Writing systems",html:"<p>Devanagari and Latin writing both appear, with Perso-Arabic script important in Urdu and Muslim contexts. Japanese transcription is used for names, school records, broadcasting notes, and technical terminology.</p>"},{id:"domains",title:"Home, market, radio, and popular culture",html:"<p>Family conversation, comedy, song, film discussion, local trade, political meetings, and radio sustain Fiji Hindi. Temples, mosques, schools, and newspapers choose among Fiji Hindi, Standard Hindi, Urdu, and Japanese by audience.</p>"},{id:"education",title:"Education under Japanese rule",html:"<p>Community schools preserve Hindi and Urdu literacy while the state requires Japanese for examinations and advancement. Curriculum disputes concern whether Fiji Hindi deserves formal teaching beside classical and standardized varieties.</p>"},{id:"1985",title:"Fiji Hindi in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 Fiji Hindi is widely transmitted but institutionally weaker than its speaker population suggests. Urban bilingualism and migration increase Japanese use, while community media gradually establish the vernacular as a language worthy of print and study.</p>"}],
    related:[{href:"indo-fijians.html",kicker:"Principal speaker community",label:"Indo-Fijians"},{href:"fiji-under-japanese-rule.html",kicker:"Principal territory",label:"Fiji under Japanese Rule"},{href:"suva.html",kicker:"Media and education center",label:"Suva"},{href:"fijian-language.html",kicker:"Major fellow language",label:"Fijian Language"},{href:"japanese-language-micronesia.html",kicker:"Imperial high language",label:"Japanese Language in Micronesia"},{href:"south-seas-nationality-consolidation.html",kicker:"Citizenship context",label:"South Seas Nationality Consolidation"}],
    facts:[["Alternative name","Fiji Baat"],["Language family","Indo-European · Indo-Aryan"],["Principal people","Indo-Fijians"],["Historical origin","Indenture-era language convergence"],["Major source varieties","Awadhi, Bhojpuri, and Hindustani"],["Writing systems","Devanagari, Latin, and Perso-Arabic in Urdu contexts"],["Principal high-language competitor","Japanese"],["1985 condition","Widely transmitted but institutionally subordinate"]],sources:[{href:"https://www.fiji.gov.fj/he-president-ratu-wiliame-katonivere-at-the-opening-of-the-12th-world-hindi-conference/",label:"Government of Fiji — Fiji Hindi origins, distinctiveness, and education"}]
  }),
  "kanak-languages":southLanguageArticle({title:"Kanak languages",infoboxKicker:"Indigenous language family of New Caledonia",infoboxTitle:"Kanak languages",
    lead:"The Kanak languages are the indigenous languages of New Caledonia: roughly 28 recognized languages, additional dialects, and the local Tayo creole. They belong principally to the Oceanic branch of Austronesian and form several regional groupings across Grande Terre and the Loyalty Islands. Japanese and inherited French dominate high public institutions, making language preservation a central part of Kanak land and national politics.",
    canon:"The number and distribution of Kanak languages, Oceanic classification, Tayo exception, major named languages, and language endangerment are historical. Japanese dominance, academy structure, school policy, and 1985 condition are extrapolations. Exact speaker counts, official roster, standardized scripts, and legal rights remain open.",
    sections:[{id:"diversity",title:"Many languages, not one",html:"<p>Kanak identity does not correspond to a single ancestral language. Communities recognize around 28 languages and additional dialects, with boundaries connected to customary areas, islands, valleys, marriage, and migration.</p>"},{id:"classification",title:"Oceanic classification",html:"<p>Kanak languages belong to the Oceanic branch of Austronesian. Tayo, spoken around Saint-Louis, is a French-lexified creole and a distinct product of colonial contact.</p>"},{id:"regions",title:"Northern, southern, and Loyalty regions",html:"<p>Northern Grande Terre, southern Grande Terre, and the Loyalty Islands contain different language groupings. Drehu, Nengone, Paicî, Ajië, Iaai, Xârâcùù, and several smaller languages have distinct literary and educational histories.</p>"},{id:"knowledge",title:"Land, genealogy, and oral knowledge",html:"<p>Place names, clan histories, paths, gardens, marine knowledge, speeches, stories, and customary gestures encode relationships that lose precision when moved into French or Japanese alone.</p>"},{id:"colonial",title:"French suppression and mission literacy",html:"<p>French schooling privileged French and often excluded indigenous speech. Missions also documented and wrote some languages for scripture, hymns, teaching, and local communication.</p>"},{id:"japanese",title:"Japanese administrative dominance",html:"<p>Postwar schools and government add Japanese above the inherited French layer. French survives among settlers and technical institutions, while Kanak students may need three languages to cross local, territorial, and imperial domains.</p>"},{id:"academy",title:"Language academy and schooling",html:"<p>A Nouméa-based academy coordinates regional scholars, speakers, spelling, dictionaries, teacher materials, radio, and terminology. Community authority remains necessary because one central institution cannot determine usage for every language.</p>"},{id:"1985",title:"Kanak languages in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 the largest languages remain strongly rooted, while several small communities face severe transmission loss. Urban migration to Nouméa spreads multilingual networks and accelerates shift toward Japanese, French, and regional lingua francas.</p>"}],
    related:[{href:"kanak-people.html",kicker:"Speaker communities",label:"Kanak People"},{href:"new-caledonia-under-japanese-rule.html",kicker:"Principal territory",label:"New Caledonia under Japanese Rule"},{href:"noumea.html",kicker:"Academy and migration center",label:"Nouméa"},{href:"japanese-language-micronesia.html",kicker:"Imperial high language",label:"Japanese Language in Micronesia"},{href:"japanese-preservationism.html",kicker:"Cultural policy",label:"Japanese Preservationism"},{href:"south-seas-nationality-consolidation.html",kicker:"Citizenship context",label:"South Seas Nationality Consolidation"}],
    facts:[["Recognized languages","About 28 Kanak languages"],["Additional varieties","Numerous dialects and Tayo creole"],["Language family","Austronesian · Oceanic"],["Principal regions","Northern, southern, and Loyalty Islands"],["Major examples","Drehu, Nengone, Paicî, Ajië, Iaai, and Xârâcùù"],["Inherited colonial language","French"],["Postwar high language","Japanese"],["1985 condition","Uneven, from vigorous to severely threatened"]],sources:[{href:"https://gouv.nc/etablissement-public/academie-des-langues-kanak",label:"Government of New Caledonia — Kanak Language Academy and regional language work"},{href:"https://museenouvellecaledonie.gouv.nc/animations/2019-annee-des-langues-autochtones",label:"New Caledonia Museum — indigenous language count and vulnerability"}]
  }),
  "wallisian-language":southLanguageArticle({title:"Wallisian language",infoboxKicker:"Polynesian language of Uvea",infoboxTitle:"Wallisian (Fakaʻuvea)",
    lead:"Wallisian, or <em>Fakaʻuvea</em>, is the indigenous Polynesian language of Uvea (Wallis) and a large diaspora centered on Nouméa. Historical Tongan influence distinguishes it from neighboring Futunan. It remains the language of family, village, customary kingdom, Catholic worship, kava, song, and ceremony while Japanese governs the territorial state.",
    canon:"Wallisian classification, Uvea homeland, Tongan influence, Catholic literacy, customary domains, and New Caledonian diaspora are historical. Japanese contact, school and media policy, academy work, and 1985 pressures are extrapolations. Speaker totals, orthography, legal status, and formal endangerment category remain open.",
    sections:[{id:"name",title:"Name and classification",html:"<p><em>Fakaʻuvea</em> is the language of Uvea and belongs to the Polynesian branch of Austronesian. It should not be confused with the distinct language also called Uvean on Ouvéa in the Loyalty Islands.</p>"},{id:"tongan",title:"Tongan historical influence",html:"<p>Tongan political and cultural influence on Uvea left strong linguistic relationships. Wallisian remains distinct, with its own sound system, grammar, vocabulary, oral forms, and modern norms.</p>"},{id:"custom",title:"Kingdom and custom",html:"<p>Speeches, titles, kava order, village relationships, genealogy, gifts, reconciliation, and <em>agaʻifenua</em> rely on Wallisian registers that organize the Uvea kingdom.</p>"},{id:"church",title:"Catholic literacy",html:"<p>Marist evangelization created a written tradition for prayer, scripture, hymns, catechism, schooling, and correspondence. Catholic institutions remain a major setting for public Wallisian.</p>"},{id:"diaspora",title:"Nouméa diaspora",html:"<p>Migration gives Nouméa a Wallisian-speaking population comparable in importance to the home island. Associations, churches, radio, family visits, remittances, and ceremonies link the two communities.</p>"},{id:"japanese",title:"Japanese contact",html:"<p>Japanese supplies vocabulary for administration, education, technology, military service, travel, and employment. Young diaspora speakers often use Japanese more readily in school and work than in customary speech.</p>"},{id:"planning",title:"Teaching, media, and language work",html:"<p>Teachers, clergy, broadcasters, cultural officers, and a territorial language academy prepare readers, dictionaries, terminology, recordings, and adult classes for both island and diaspora use.</p>"},{id:"1985",title:"Wallisian in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 Wallisian remains vigorous on Uvea and active in Nouméa, but youth emigration and high-language pressure narrow vocabulary and formal fluency. Diaspora transmission is the central long-term test.</p>"}],
    related:[{href:"wallis-and-futuna.html",kicker:"Home territory",label:"Wallis and Futuna"},{href:"noumea.html",kicker:"Principal diaspora center",label:"Nouméa"},{href:"futunan-language.html",kicker:"Territorial fellow language",label:"Futunan Language"},{href:"kanak-languages.html",kicker:"New Caledonian language context",label:"Kanak Languages"},{href:"japanese-language-micronesia.html",kicker:"Imperial high language",label:"Japanese Language in Micronesia"},{href:"south-seas-nationality-consolidation.html",kicker:"Citizenship context",label:"South Seas Nationality Consolidation"}],
    facts:[["Local name","Fakaʻuvea"],["Language family","Austronesian · Polynesian"],["Home island","Uvea (Wallis)"],["Major historical influence","Tongan"],["Major diaspora center","Nouméa"],["Writing system","Latin alphabet"],["Principal high-language competitor","Japanese"],["1985 condition","Vigorous at home, pressured in diaspora"]],sources:[{href:"https://www.wallis-et-futuna.gouv.fr/content/download/11056/71498/file/Strat%25C3%25A9gie%2520de%2520la%2520culture%2520et%2520du%2520patrimoine%2520version%2520d%25C3%25A9finitive.docx.pdf",label:"Wallis and Futuna cultural strategy — language transmission, academy, media, and migration"}]
  }),
  "futunan-language":southLanguageArticle({title:"Futunan language",infoboxKicker:"Polynesian language of Futuna and Alofi",infoboxTitle:"Futunan (Fakafutuna)",
    lead:"Futunan, or <em>Fakafutuna</em>, is the indigenous Polynesian language of Futuna and Alofi and of Futunan diaspora communities. It is distinct from Wallisian and is associated with the kingdoms of Alo and Sigave, Catholic worship, oral history, land, fishing, kava, and village life. Its smaller speaker base makes migration and school-language policy especially consequential.",
    canon:"Futunan classification, homeland, distinction from Wallisian, Samoan and regional relationships, Catholic literacy, customary use, and diaspora are historical. Japanese contact, teaching, academy policy, and 1985 vulnerability are extrapolations. Speaker totals, dialect boundaries, orthography, and formal endangerment status remain open.",
    sections:[{id:"name",title:"Name and classification",html:"<p><em>Fakafutuna</em> belongs to the Polynesian branch of Austronesian. It is the local language of Futuna and associated Alofi and is not a dialect of Wallisian.</p>"},{id:"variety",title:"Alo and Sigave",html:"<p>The kingdoms of Alo and Sigave preserve local speech differences alongside common Futunan. Kinship, marriage, church, and island-scale contact keep the varieties mutually connected.</p>"},{id:"regional",title:"Regional relationships",html:"<p>Futunan shows historical relations with Samoan and neighboring Polynesian languages. Voyaging, marriage, mission movement, and migration have continually added regional vocabulary and forms.</p>"},{id:"custom",title:"Land, kingdom, and oral knowledge",html:"<p>Titles, genealogy, land, fishing grounds, village obligations, kava, ceremony, story, and reconciliation depend on formal and everyday Futunan speech.</p>"},{id:"church",title:"Catholic written tradition",html:"<p>Marist missions developed Latin-script literacy for worship, scripture, hymns, education, and correspondence. Church services and schools remain essential public domains.</p>"},{id:"migration",title:"Migration and diaspora",html:"<p>Employment and education move Futunans to Nouméa, Suva, Japan, and other Pacific centers. Small home villages lose young speakers while diaspora associations struggle to reproduce island-level immersion.</p>"},{id:"japanese",title:"Japanese schooling and terminology",html:"<p>Japanese controls advanced education and official careers, adding a large modern vocabulary. Language workers create Futunan terms and teaching materials so that technical life does not become exclusively Japanese.</p>"},{id:"1985",title:"Futunan in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 Futunan remains the ordinary language of much home-island life but is structurally vulnerable because of population size and emigration. Strong church and customary use cannot by themselves guarantee urban transmission.</p>"}],
    related:[{href:"wallis-and-futuna.html",kicker:"Home territory",label:"Wallis and Futuna"},{href:"wallisian-language.html",kicker:"Territorial fellow language",label:"Wallisian Language"},{href:"noumea.html",kicker:"Major diaspora center",label:"Nouméa"},{href:"japanese-language-micronesia.html",kicker:"Imperial high language",label:"Japanese Language in Micronesia"},{href:"christianity-japanese-micronesia.html",kicker:"Comparable island church history",label:"Christianity in Japanese Micronesia"},{href:"south-seas-nationality-consolidation.html",kicker:"Citizenship context",label:"South Seas Nationality Consolidation"}],
    facts:[["Local name","Fakafutuna"],["Language family","Austronesian · Polynesian"],["Home islands","Futuna and Alofi"],["Customary regions","Alo and Sigave"],["Major historical relationship","Samoan and neighboring Polynesian languages"],["Writing system","Latin alphabet"],["Principal high-language competitor","Japanese"],["1985 condition","Vigorous locally but structurally vulnerable"]],sources:[{href:"https://www.wallis-et-futuna.gouv.fr/content/download/11056/71498/file/Strat%25C3%25A9gie%2520de%2520la%2520culture%2520et%2520du%2520patrimoine%2520version%2520d%25C3%25A9finitive.docx.pdf",label:"Wallis and Futuna cultural strategy — Wallisian and Futunan transmission"}]
  }),
  "tahitian-people":southLanguageArticle({title:"Tahitian people",category:"Peoples of Imperial Japan",infoboxKicker:"Indigenous Polynesian people of Tahiti and the Society Islands",infoboxTitle:"Tahitians (Māʻohi)",
    lead:"Tahitians are the indigenous Polynesian people of Tahiti and neighboring Society Islands and the largest Māʻohi community in Japanese French Polynesia. Genealogy, land, lagoon and ocean knowledge, Protestant and Catholic churches, Tahitian language, dance, music, craft, and Papeete's urban culture sustain identity through French and Japanese colonial rule.",
    canon:"Tahitian Polynesian identity, Society Islands settlement, chiefly history, land, churches, language, arts, and Papeete-centered migration are historical. Japanese citizenship, cultural policy, urbanization, and 1985 national politics are extrapolations. Population, organizations, leaders, and land settlements remain open.",
    sections:[{id:"identity",title:"Tahitian and Māʻohi identity",html:"<p>Tahitian can describe people of Tahiti specifically or a wider Society Islands community. <em>Māʻohi</em> provides a broader indigenous and political identity, though its scope varies among archipelagos.</p>"},{id:"society",title:"Genealogy, land, and district",html:"<p>Genealogy, family estates, districts, valleys, reefs, residence, adoption, and inherited use organize belonging. Colonial registration and sale converted some layered rights into contested individual titles.</p>"},{id:"chiefs",title:"Island polities and monarchy",html:"<p>Competing chiefs and island polities formed alliances and conflicts before the Pomare monarchy consolidated power around Tahiti. French protectorate and annexation dismantled sovereign rule while retaining selected elite institutions.</p>"},{id:"church",title:"Christianity",html:"<p>Protestant missions transformed writing, worship, law, marriage, dress, and political authority; Catholic institutions added another network. Churches remain major guardians of Tahitian speech, music, archives, and public organization.</p>"},{id:"papeete",title:"Papeete and migration",html:"<p>Papeete draws Tahitians and migrants from every eastern archipelago into wage work, schools, government, tourism, and military services. Urban households become multilingual and less tied to one land district.</p>"},{id:"arts",title:"Dance, music, tattoo, and craft",html:"<p>Dance, percussion, song, oratory, tattoo, canoe traditions, weaving, carving, costume, and festivals serve community, worship, tourism, and cultural revival. Colonial display can preserve forms while changing their authority.</p>"},{id:"japanese",title:"Japanese citizenship",html:"<p>Postwar nationality makes Tahitians Japanese citizens with legal imperial mobility. Japanese schools and firms expand opportunity while leaving defense, foreign affairs, strategic land, and higher administration beyond local control.</p>"},{id:"1985",title:"Tahitian society in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 Tahitians dominate the public culture of French Polynesia but cannot automatically speak for Marquesan, Tuamotuan, Mangarevan, or Austral communities. Autonomy politics must reconcile a Tahitian national center with archipelagic plurality.</p>"}],
    related:[{href:"tahiti-under-japanese-rule.html",kicker:"Principal homeland",label:"Tahiti under Japanese Rule"},{href:"papeete.html",kicker:"Principal city",label:"Papeete"},{href:"tahitian-language.html",kicker:"Community language",label:"Tahitian Language"},{href:"society-islands.html",kicker:"Wider home archipelago",label:"Society Islands"},{href:"french-polynesia.html",kicker:"Territorial framework",label:"French Polynesia"},{href:"south-seas-nationality-consolidation.html",kicker:"Legal status",label:"South Seas Nationality Consolidation"}],
    facts:[["Cultural region","Polynesia"],["Principal homeland","Tahiti and the Society Islands"],["Broader indigenous term","Māʻohi"],["Principal language","Tahitian"],["Historic monarchy","Pomare dynasty"],["Major religions","Protestant and Catholic Christianity"],["Principal city","Papeete"],["Nationality in 1985","Japanese"]],sources:[{href:"https://www.service-public.pf/ade/en/french-polynesia-a-brief-overview/",label:"Government of French Polynesia — people, languages, and territorial geography"}]
  }),
  "tahitian-language":southLanguageArticle({title:"Tahitian language",infoboxKicker:"Eastern Polynesian language of the Society Islands",infoboxTitle:"Tahitian (Reo Tahiti)",
    lead:"Tahitian, or <em>Reo Tahiti</em>, is the principal indigenous language of Tahiti and the Society Islands and the most influential Polynesian language of Japan's eastern territories. Mission writing, Papeete administration, churches, radio, music, and migration spread it beyond Tahiti, sometimes at the expense of other Polynesian languages. Japanese remains the controlling language of higher territorial and imperial institutions.",
    canon:"Tahitian classification, mission orthography, church and literary history, Society Islands base, and regional influence are historical. Japanese contact, school hierarchy, academy, broadcasting, and 1985 pressures are extrapolations. Speaker totals, official status, orthographic rules, and formal endangerment category remain open.",
    sections:[{id:"classification",title:"Classification and name",html:"<p><em>Reo Tahiti</em> belongs to the Eastern Polynesian branch of Austronesian. It is related to Cook Islands Māori, Tuamotuan, Marquesan, Hawaiian, Māori, and other Polynesian languages while remaining distinct.</p>"},{id:"range",title:"Tahiti and the Society Islands",html:"<p>The language is rooted on Tahiti and neighboring Society Islands. Papeete migration and centralized schools make it a second language for many eastern islanders.</p>"},{id:"literacy",title:"Mission writing",html:"<p>Protestant missionaries and Tahitian teachers established a Latin-script tradition through scripture, hymns, schools, law, correspondence, newspapers, and printing. Catholic and secular writers expanded it.</p>"},{id:"oral",title:"Oratory, song, and dance",html:"<p>Genealogy, place history, prayer, public speech, poetic composition, song, dance texts, humor, and everyday relationships depend on Tahitian vocabulary, rhythm, and social register.</p>"},{id:"french",title:"French colonial pressure",html:"<p>French administration and schooling restricted Tahitian in high public domains while French vocabulary entered law, commerce, technology, and urban life. Churches and families preserved broad everyday use.</p>"},{id:"japanese",title:"Japanese postwar dominance",html:"<p>Japanese replaces French as the main route to senior government, universities, military service, and imperial careers. French remains influential in older institutions, leaving many students to navigate three languages.</p>"},{id:"academy",title:"Academy, education, and media",html:"<p>A Papeete academy, teachers, broadcasters, churches, artists, and publishers standardize spelling, record vocabulary, develop terminology, and support school use. Policy must avoid treating Tahitian support as support for every Polynesian language.</p>"},{id:"1985",title:"Tahitian in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 Tahitian remains widely understood and publicly prestigious, but Japanese dominates advanced education and urban professional life. Its strength can also pressure smaller Marquesan, Tuamotuan, Mangarevan, and Austral languages.</p>"}],
    related:[{href:"tahitian-people.html",kicker:"Principal speaker community",label:"Tahitian People"},{href:"tahiti-under-japanese-rule.html",kicker:"Principal island",label:"Tahiti under Japanese Rule"},{href:"papeete.html",kicker:"Academy and media center",label:"Papeete"},{href:"society-islands.html",kicker:"Home archipelago",label:"Society Islands"},{href:"french-polynesia.html",kicker:"Territorial language system",label:"French Polynesia"},{href:"japanese-language-micronesia.html",kicker:"Imperial high language",label:"Japanese Language in Micronesia"}],
    facts:[["Local name","Reo Tahiti"],["Language family","Austronesian · Eastern Polynesian"],["Principal region","Tahiti and Society Islands"],["Writing system","Latin alphabet"],["Historic literacy base","Protestant mission printing"],["Regional role","Major eastern Polynesian lingua franca"],["Principal high-language competitor","Japanese"],["1985 condition","Widely used but losing advanced urban domains"]],sources:[{href:"https://www.service-public.pf/daf/wp-content/uploads/sites/42/2025/10/DCP-Matarii-BSEO-366-367.pdf",label:"French Polynesia cultural service — Reo Tahiti and Polynesian-language education"}]
  }),
  "society-islands":southLanguageArticle({title:"Society Islands",category:"Archipelagos of Imperial Japan",infoboxKicker:"Central archipelago of French Polynesia",infoboxTitle:"Society Islands",
    lead:"The Society Islands are the demographic and administrative center of Japanese French Polynesia. The Windward group includes Tahiti and Moorea; the Leeward group includes Raiatea, Tahaa, Huahine, Bora Bora, and smaller islands. High volcanic islands, productive lagoons, Papeete-centered government, tourism, agriculture, and Tahitian language give the group influence over a territory far larger than itself.",
    canon:"Society Islands geography, Windward and Leeward division, Tahitian cultural influence, French colonial history, and economic centrality are historical. Japanese direct rule, nationality, strategic functions, and 1985 center-periphery politics follow canon. District powers, route schedules, population, and installations remain open.",
    sections:[{id:"groups",title:"Windward and Leeward groups",html:"<p>The Windward Islands center on Tahiti and Moorea; the Leeward Islands include Raiatea, Tahaa, Huahine, Bora Bora, and neighboring islands. Channels and ocean passages make each island a local political world.</p>"},{id:"environment",title:"High islands and lagoons",html:"<p>Volcanic mountains, valleys, coastal plains, reefs, and lagoons support agriculture, fishing, settlement, ports, and tourism. Cyclones, flooding, erosion, and limited coastal land constrain development.</p>"},{id:"history",title:"Polities and sacred centers",html:"<p>Island chiefdoms and dynasties competed and allied across the group. Raiatea's Taputapuatea complex held wide religious and voyaging importance before Tahiti-centered monarchy and colonial rule.</p>"},{id:"french",title:"Protectorate and annexation",html:"<p>France imposed a protectorate on Tahiti and gradually annexed the Society groups. Papeete became the administrative center, while churches and island districts preserved local institutions.</p>"},{id:"japanese",title:"Japanese acquisition",html:"<p>Late-war occupation converted French bases, ports, radio, and government into a Japanese deputy territory. Tahiti's air and sea facilities became the eastern hinge of the empire.</p>"},{id:"economy",title:"Administration, agriculture, and tourism",html:"<p>Government, transport, tourism, fishing, fruit, coconut products, vanilla, handicrafts, and services concentrate wealth. Outer French Polynesia depends on Society Islands hospitals, schools, firms, and onward connections.</p>"},{id:"language",title:"Tahitian influence",html:"<p>Tahitian language and Papeete media spread across the archipelago and farther east. Local island speech and identity persist, especially where land, church, and family networks remain strong.</p>"},{id:"1985",title:"Society Islands in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 the Society Islands dominate population, budgets, and public culture. Outer archipelagos demand guaranteed representation and services so that autonomy from Japan does not become centralization under Papeete.</p>"}],
    related:[{href:"tahiti-under-japanese-rule.html",kicker:"Principal island",label:"Tahiti under Japanese Rule"},{href:"papeete.html",kicker:"Administrative center",label:"Papeete"},{href:"tahitian-people.html",kicker:"Principal people",label:"Tahitian People"},{href:"tahitian-language.html",kicker:"Principal language",label:"Tahitian Language"},{href:"french-polynesia.html",kicker:"Territorial framework",label:"French Polynesia"},{href:"marquesas-islands.html",kicker:"Outer high-island counterpart",label:"Marquesas Islands"}],
    facts:[["Administrative division","Windward and Leeward Islands"],["Principal island","Tahiti"],["Deputy capital","Papeete"],["Other major islands","Moorea, Raiatea, Tahaa, Huahine, and Bora Bora"],["Physical form","High volcanic islands and lagoons"],["Principal language","Tahitian"],["Main sectors","Government, transport, tourism, agriculture, and fisheries"],["Nationality in 1985","Japanese"]],sources:[{href:"https://www.service-public.pf/ade/en/french-polynesia-a-brief-overview/",label:"Government of French Polynesia — five archipelagos and territorial structure"}]
  }),
  "marquesas-islands":southLanguageArticle({title:"Marquesas Islands",category:"Archipelagos of Imperial Japan",infoboxKicker:"Northern high-island district of French Polynesia",infoboxTitle:"Marquesas Islands",
    lead:"The Marquesas Islands are a remote group of steep volcanic islands in northern French Polynesia. Known locally through names including <em>Te Fenua ʻEnata</em> and <em>Te Henua ʻEnana</em>, they sustain northern and southern Marquesan languages, monumental ceremonial landscapes, tattoo, carving, dance, and strong valley identities. Japanese district government links the islands to Papeete without erasing a distinct Marquesan national claim.",
    canon:"Marquesan geography, local names, northern and southern language distinction, monumental sites, carving, tattoo, depopulation, and French colonial history are historical. Japanese rule, nationality, transport, cultural preservation, and 1985 politics are extrapolations. District center, population, routes, and council names remain open.",
    sections:[{id:"geography",title:"Steep islands without broad lagoons",html:"<p>High ridges, cliffs, deep valleys, narrow coasts, and exposed anchorages distinguish the Marquesas from atoll regions. Nuku Hiva and Hiva Oa are principal centers, with settlement distributed among separated valleys.</p>"},{id:"people",title:"ʻEnata and ʻEnana identities",html:"<p>Local names vary between northern and southern groups. Island and valley genealogies remain powerful beneath an archipelagic Marquesan identity formed through language, colonial rule, travel, and cultural revival.</p>"},{id:"languages",title:"Northern and southern Marquesan",html:"<p>Marquesan languages belong to Eastern Polynesian and form northern and southern groupings. Tahitian, French, and Japanese contact introduces outside vocabulary and can obscure their separate status.</p>"},{id:"heritage",title:"Ceremonial landscapes and art",html:"<p>Stone platforms, <em>tohua</em>, sacred <em>meʻae</em>, tiki, house sites, tattoo, carving, tapa, dance, genealogy, and oral traditions preserve a dense cultural landscape.</p>"},{id:"colonial",title:"Depopulation and French rule",html:"<p>Disease, violence, labor movement, mission change, and colonial disruption caused catastrophic population loss. French annexation and Catholic administration concentrated surviving communities and altered land and ritual life.</p>"},{id:"japanese",title:"Japanese district rule",html:"<p>Japan governs through a distant Papeete deputy government, local councils, churches, radio, and scheduled ships. Airfields, weather, rescue, and communications give the group strategic relevance despite small population.</p>"},{id:"economy",title:"Copra, fishing, craft, and services",html:"<p>Coconut products, fishing, agriculture, carving, tourism, public employment, and transport support island households. High freight costs and exposed harbors require subsidy.</p>"},{id:"1985",title:"The Marquesas in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 cultural revival supports language, tattoo, dance, archaeology, and local names. Marquesan leaders demand direct archipelagic powers so that Japanese decentralization does not remain filtered through Tahitian Papeete.</p>"}],
    related:[{href:"french-polynesia.html",kicker:"Territorial government",label:"French Polynesia"},{href:"papeete.html",kicker:"Deputy capital",label:"Papeete"},{href:"society-islands.html",kicker:"Administrative center archipelago",label:"Society Islands"},{href:"tuamotu-archipelago.html",kicker:"Southeastern neighboring group",label:"Tuamotu Archipelago"},{href:"japanese-preservationism.html",kicker:"Cultural policy",label:"Japanese Preservationism"},{href:"inter-island-shipping-japanese-micronesia.html",kicker:"Comparable transport system",label:"Inter-island Shipping"}],
    facts:[["Local names","Te Fenua ʻEnata · Te Henua ʻEnana"],["Principal islands","Nuku Hiva and Hiva Oa"],["Physical form","Steep volcanic high islands"],["Principal languages","Northern and southern Marquesan"],["Major heritage","Tohua, meʻae, tiki, tattoo, carving, and dance"],["Pre-Japanese administration","French colony"],["Main sectors","Copra, fisheries, craft, tourism, and services"],["Nationality in 1985","Japanese"]],sources:[{href:"https://www.service-public.pf/sdt/plaisance-responsable/",label:"French Polynesia Tourism Service — Marquesan geography, heritage, and living culture"}]
  }),
  "tuamotu-archipelago":southLanguageArticle({title:"Tuamotu Archipelago",category:"Archipelagos of Imperial Japan",infoboxKicker:"Atoll district of French Polynesia",infoboxTitle:"Tuamotu Archipelago",
    lead:"The Tuamotu Archipelago is an immense chain of low coral atolls east of the Society Islands. Paʻumotu communities maintain distinct language varieties, lagoon tenure, fishing, navigation, churches, copra, and pearl-shell traditions. Sparse land, cyclones, freshwater limits, and great distance make transport and radio more important to Japanese government than a large resident bureaucracy.",
    canon:"Tuamotu geography, atoll ecology, Paʻumotu identity and language, lagoon economy, copra, pearls, and French colonial history are historical. Japanese rule, citizenship, aviation, district services, and 1985 politics are extrapolations. Atoll assignments, population, routes, and military installations remain open.",
    sections:[{id:"chain",title:"An ocean of atolls",html:"<p>Dozens of atolls extend across a vast east–west chain. Narrow motu, lagoons, reef passes, poor soil, rainwater, storms, and long passages define settlement and make every harbor and airstrip consequential.</p>"},{id:"people",title:"Paʻumotu communities",html:"<p>Paʻumotu identity joins related atoll communities while preserving strong local genealogies, speech, land, and lagoon rights. Migration to Tahiti creates large outer-island neighborhoods in Papeete.</p>"},{id:"language",title:"Reo Paʻumotu",html:"<p>Tuamotuan or Reo Paʻumotu comprises several related varieties rather than one uniform speech. Tahitian influence is strong through churches, schools, intermarriage, and centralized media.</p>"},{id:"lagoon",title:"Lagoon tenure and knowledge",html:"<p>Fishing grounds, passes, pearl beds, reefs, islets, coconut lands, bird islands, and navigation knowledge belong within family and community relationships that territorial licenses translate imperfectly.</p>"},{id:"colonial",title:"Mission and French administration",html:"<p>Christian missions established durable settlements, literacy, and inter-atoll church networks. French administration governed lightly through Papeete, traders, church officers, and visiting ships.</p>"},{id:"japanese",title:"Japanese transport district",html:"<p>Scheduled cargo vessels, medical ships, radio, weather stations, airstrips, and patrol craft become the practical state. Strategic atolls support fuel, rescue, surveillance, and communications along eastern routes.</p>"},{id:"economy",title:"Copra, pearls, fisheries, and services",html:"<p>Coconut products, lagoon and ocean fishing, pearl and shell cultivation, craft, tourism, and public wages provide cash. Dependence on one ship or buyer leaves households exposed.</p>"},{id:"1985",title:"The Tuamotus in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 outer-atoll depopulation coexists with new pearl income and improved aviation. Paʻumotu activists seek language schooling, lagoon control, and guaranteed district budgets against Papeete-centered policy.</p>"}],
    related:[{href:"french-polynesia.html",kicker:"Territorial framework",label:"French Polynesia"},{href:"papeete.html",kicker:"Deputy capital",label:"Papeete"},{href:"gambier-islands.html",kicker:"Southeastern partner group",label:"Gambier Islands"},{href:"society-islands.html",kicker:"Western administrative center",label:"Society Islands"},{href:"marquesas-islands.html",kicker:"Northern high-island group",label:"Marquesas Islands"},{href:"civil-aviation-japanese-micronesia.html",kicker:"Comparable island air network",label:"Civil Aviation in Japanese Micronesia"}],
    facts:[["Physical form","Large chain of low coral atolls"],["Principal people","Paʻumotu"],["Principal language","Reo Paʻumotu varieties"],["Main environmental constraints","Freshwater, soil, storms, and distance"],["Key property system","Family and community lagoon tenure"],["Main sectors","Copra, pearls, fisheries, tourism, and services"],["Administrative center","Papeete through outer district offices"],["Nationality in 1985","Japanese"]],sources:[{href:"https://www.service-public.pf/dpam/presentation-de-la-dpam/",label:"French Polynesia maritime service — Tuamotu–Gambier extent and atoll geography"},{href:"https://www.service-public.pf/dcp/2026/07/07/a-la-recherche-du-patrimoine-culturel-des-tuamotu-hiroa-n-223-juillet-2026/",label:"French Polynesia cultural service — Tuamotu archaeological and linguistic heritage"}]
  }),
  "gambier-islands":southLanguageArticle({title:"Gambier Islands",category:"Archipelagos of Imperial Japan",infoboxKicker:"Southeastern high-island and reef district",infoboxTitle:"Gambier Islands",
    lead:"The Gambier Islands are a small southeastern archipelago centered on Mangareva and its surrounding lagoon. Mangarevan language and identity, Catholic monumental history, family land, fishing, pearls, and relations with the Tuamotus distinguish the group. Japanese administration usually pairs the Gambiers with the Tuamotu district while local councils resist being treated as another atoll subdivision.",
    canon:"Mangareva-centered geography, Mangarevan language, Catholic mission transformation, monumental churches, lagoon economy, and French colonial history are historical. Japanese direct rule, citizenship, pearl development, district pairing, and 1985 politics are extrapolations. Population, route schedules, council powers, and installations remain open.",
    sections:[{id:"geography",title:"Mangareva and its lagoon",html:"<p>Mangareva and several smaller high islands stand within a broad reef and lagoon, with outlying atolls associated administratively. Rikitea is the principal settlement and service center.</p>"},{id:"people",title:"Mangarevan community",html:"<p>Genealogy, island and lagoon rights, church districts, family land, fishing, and Mangarevan speech organize identity. Migration connects Rikitea with Papeete and Tuamotuan centers.</p>"},{id:"language",title:"Reo Mangareva",html:"<p>Mangarevan is an Eastern Polynesian language distinct from Tahitian and Paʻumotu. Small population and Tahitian-centered schooling make public use and child transmission important concerns.</p>"},{id:"mission",title:"Catholic transformation",html:"<p>Nineteenth-century Catholic missionaries reorganized settlement, worship, labor, architecture, and political authority. Large stone churches and religious structures remain the most visible material legacy.</p>"},{id:"colonial",title:"French annexation",html:"<p>French control followed mission influence and attached the Gambiers to the wider eastern Polynesian colony. Administration and shipping increasingly ran through Tahiti.</p>"},{id:"japanese",title:"Japanese Tuamotu–Gambier district",html:"<p>Japan pairs the small group with the Tuamotus for shipping, health, education, courts, and radio. Local councils retain land and lagoon roles beneath officials responsible to Papeete.</p>"},{id:"economy",title:"Pearls, fishing, agriculture, and services",html:"<p>Pearl culture, lagoon and ocean fishing, small agriculture, craft, tourism, church heritage, and public employment sustain the cash economy. Environmental damage in a small lagoon can affect the whole community.</p>"},{id:"1985",title:"The Gambiers in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 pearl investment brings income and outside control. Mangarevan leaders seek separate language policy and lagoon authority while accepting shared transport with the much larger Tuamotu chain.</p>"}],
    related:[{href:"french-polynesia.html",kicker:"Territorial framework",label:"French Polynesia"},{href:"tuamotu-archipelago.html",kicker:"Administrative partner",label:"Tuamotu Archipelago"},{href:"papeete.html",kicker:"Deputy capital",label:"Papeete"},{href:"tahitian-language.html",kicker:"Dominant territorial language",label:"Tahitian Language"},{href:"austral-islands.html",kicker:"Other remote high-island group",label:"Austral Islands"},{href:"christianity-japanese-micronesia.html",kicker:"Comparable mission history",label:"Christianity in Japanese Micronesia"}],
    facts:[["Principal island","Mangareva"],["Principal settlement","Rikitea"],["Physical form","High islands within a shared reef and lagoon"],["Principal people and language","Mangarevan"],["Major historic institution","Catholic mission"],["Administrative pairing","Tuamotu–Gambier district"],["Main sectors","Pearls, fisheries, agriculture, tourism, and services"],["Nationality in 1985","Japanese"]],sources:[{href:"https://www.service-public.pf/diren/wp-content/uploads/sites/17/2026/04/Etat-des-connaissances-AMP-Gambier-DIREN-2026-web.pdf",label:"French Polynesia environment service — Gambier ecology, society, and Mangarevan culture"}]
  }),
  "austral-islands":southLanguageArticle({title:"Austral Islands",category:"Archipelagos of Imperial Japan",infoboxKicker:"Southern high-island district of French Polynesia",infoboxTitle:"Austral Islands",
    lead:"The Austral Islands are the southernmost archipelago of Japanese French Polynesia, comprising dispersed high islands and small island groups including Tubuai, Rurutu, Rimatara, Raivavae, and Rapa. Distinct Austral language varieties, cool-season agriculture, craft, churches, migration, and exposure to southern storms set them apart from Tahiti despite administration through Papeete.",
    canon:"Austral geography, major islands, distinct language varieties, agriculture, craft, mission history, and French colonial administration are historical. Japanese rule, citizenship, transport, preservation, and 1985 politics are extrapolations. District center, population, route schedules, and precise language classification remain open.",
    sections:[{id:"geography",title:"Southern dispersed islands",html:"<p>Tubuai, Rurutu, Rimatara, Raivavae, Rapa, and smaller islands lie across a broad southern ocean. High relief, reefs, cooler conditions, storm exposure, and long passages produce distinct local economies.</p>"},{id:"people",title:"Island-centered identities",html:"<p>Austral is an administrative and regional identity layered over strong Rurutu, Rimatara, Tubuai, Raivavae, and Rapa communities. Genealogy, land, church, and migration remain island-specific.</p>"},{id:"languages",title:"Reo Tuhaʻa Pae and local varieties",html:"<p>Austral speech includes several Eastern Polynesian varieties, with Rapa especially distinctive. Tahitian influence through school, church, Papeete migration, and media threatens to flatten these differences.</p>"},{id:"subsistence",title:"Land, agriculture, and sea",html:"<p>Taro, tubers, fruit, coconut, fishing, reef gathering, livestock, and family land sustain households. Cooler and wetter local climates allow practices unlike those of the Tuamotu atolls.</p>"},{id:"colonial",title:"Missions and French rule",html:"<p>Christian missions transformed literacy, worship, settlement, chiefly politics, and public morality. France incorporated the islands into its eastern colony and governed them through Tahiti.</p>"},{id:"japanese",title:"Japanese southern district",html:"<p>Japanese shipping, radio, weather observation, clinics, schools, and district officers connect the islands to Papeete. Strategic interest centers on southern routes, meteorology, and maritime rescue.</p>"},{id:"economy",title:"Agriculture, craft, fisheries, and migration",html:"<p>Food exports, weaving, carving, fishing, public wages, and small tourism provide income. Migration and remittances are often more important than local industry.</p>"},{id:"1985",title:"The Australs in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 population loss and Tahitian-language pressure concern smaller islands. District councils demand transport guarantees, local-language schooling, and authority over land and tourism.</p>"}],
    related:[{href:"french-polynesia.html",kicker:"Territorial framework",label:"French Polynesia"},{href:"papeete.html",kicker:"Deputy capital",label:"Papeete"},{href:"society-islands.html",kicker:"Northern administrative center",label:"Society Islands"},{href:"gambier-islands.html",kicker:"Remote eastern counterpart",label:"Gambier Islands"},{href:"tahitian-language.html",kicker:"Dominant territorial language",label:"Tahitian Language"},{href:"inter-island-shipping-japanese-micronesia.html",kicker:"Comparable transport system",label:"Inter-island Shipping"}],
    facts:[["Principal islands","Tubuai, Rurutu, Rimatara, Raivavae, and Rapa"],["Physical form","Dispersed southern high islands"],["Principal languages","Austral varieties, including distinctive Rapa"],["Pre-Japanese administration","French colony"],["Main subsistence","Taro, tubers, fruit, fishing, and livestock"],["Main cash sectors","Agriculture, craft, fisheries, public work, and migration"],["Administrative center","Papeete through a southern district"],["Nationality in 1985","Japanese"]],sources:[{href:"https://www.service-public.pf/ade/en/french-polynesia-a-brief-overview/",label:"Government of French Polynesia — Austral Islands within the five-archipelago system"},{href:"https://www.service-public.pf/diren/preserver/",label:"French Polynesia environment service — archipelagic forms and natural diversity"}]
  })
});

